# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: deferred-dialect-warning-rules-section-7 -- what MR !509 left undone, and where section 7 disagrees with the existing rules
#+STATUS: DRAFT
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: design
#+DEPENDS: error-processing-per-dialect
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-11-04

DRAFT for pjb to revise. It was written while finishing
encoding-replacement-character-option (MR !509, clautolisp / alfe 2.3.89). It
collects (A) what that MR did not implement and (B) where pjb's section 7 of
error-processing-per-dialect.issue ("Reminder for dialect warning rules and
clautolisp behavior") disagrees with section 4 of the same issue, with
AGENTS.md, or with the warnings clautolisp already emits. Section 7 is on
pjb's local master only (commit 9cf7f953, not pushed at the time of writing).

* A. Not implemented by MR !509

** A.1 CHR / ASCII / VL-STRING->LIST under the vendor dialects (rows 102, 542, 544, 545)

Section 7 says each dialect gives its product's behaviour. MR !509 covers
only the WRITE side: what WRITE-LINE, WRITE-CHAR, PRINC, PRIN1, PRINT and
PROMPT put in a file or on the terminal for a character the encoding cannot
hold. The character values themselves are unchanged:

| expression                     | AutoCAD-2022 | BricsCAD-V25 Windows | BricsCAD-V26 macOS | clautolisp, every dialect |
|--------------------------------+--------------+----------------------+--------------------+---------------------------|
| =(ascii (chr 300))=            | 44           | 73                   | 300                | 300                       |
| =(vl-string->list (chr 8364))= | (172)        | (128)                | (8364)             | (8364)                    |

Under --dialect autocad and --dialect bricscad (Windows platform) clautolisp
therefore differs from the product. Doing it means deciding where the
narrowing happens: in CHR (the string never holds the code point), or in
every reader of a string (ASCII, VL-STRING->LIST, SUBSTR ...). The first is
simpler and matches what the products appear to do, but it is a decision.
It also needs its own warning tag, or a reuse of [encoding-replacement].

** A.2 Decoding

Out of scope by the parent's instruction. A byte the encoding cannot DECODE
on READ-LINE / READ-CHAR / LOAD keeps its present behaviour. Not measured on
the vendors.

** A.3 SPEC-UNCERTAIN points (in deferred-spec-research.issue, "Encoding replacement")

- the BricsCAD best-fit table: clautolisp derives it from Unicode character
  names (LATIN ... LETTER X WITH ...). Windows' WideCharToMultiByte table is
  larger (ligatures, typographic quotes, dashes);
- what AutoCAD writes for a character above U+FFFF (two UTF-16 units, two low
  bytes?);
- what BricsCAD V26 on Windows writes (V26 was measured on macOS only);
- whether PROMPT and the command line follow the file rule.

** A.4 Terminal input and alfe's CAD backends

-Ereplacement is forwarded to the clautolisp engine only. Under --autocad /
--bricscad alfe ignores it with a warning: the CADs' own writes cannot be
changed from outside.

** A.5 Known limitation of the implementation

The "first time per caller" record (=*encoding-replacement-warnings-seen*=) is
a process-wide table. [vendor-operator] keeps its table in the runtime session.
Two engines in one process (alfe :direct running several sessions) would
share the first; a reset-default-evaluation-context does not clear it.
Should move to the session when B.3 is settled.

* B. Inconsistencies

** B.1 --strict: signal, or answer like --clautolisp?

- encoding-replacement-character-option.issue (as filed): "--strict keeps
  signalling".
- Section 7: "--strict warns, and aim to return the most common answer (or
  the most practical answer, the same as --clautolisp dialect)".
- MR !509 followed section 7: --strict writes \U+XXXX and warns.

The section 7 table then says "signal an error" for 2.1-2.4, which is the
clautolisp column. Is that also the --strict behaviour? "The most common
answer" has no meaning when one product signals and the other returns a value
(2.2, 2.3): there is no majority with two products. Proposal: say in section 7
that --strict ALWAYS behaves as --clautolisp, plus the warning, and drop "the
most common answer".

** B.2 Who is silent: AGENTS.md versus section 7

- AGENTS.md, Dialect Divergence and Warnings, case 2 (resolved divergence):
  "The adopted-vendor dialect and clautolisp perform the normative behaviour
  silently"; only --strict and the deviant vendor's dialect warn.
- AGENTS.md, case 3 (unresolved): every dialect but --lax warns.
- Section 7: every dialect but --lax warns, without distinguishing case 2
  from case 3.

So, by section 7, --dialect clautolisp warns even where the spec has adopted
one vendor. MR !509 followed section 7 (clautolisp warns). Either AGENTS.md
and autolisp-spec chapter 25 are updated to drop the "adopted vendor is
silent" rule, or section 7 is read as applying to case 3 only. Encoding is a
three-way divergence with no adopted vendor, so it is case 3 either way and
MR !509 is right under both readings; the question matters for the other
families.

** B.3 "The first time the caller called the function" versus the existing emitters

Section 7 asks for one warning per (caller, function). The existing emitters
do something different each:

| tag                    | silent under               | deduplication                         | honours *AUTOLISP-WARN-OUT-OF-DIALECT* |
|------------------------+----------------------------+---------------------------------------+----------------------------------------|
| [errno-setvar]         | --clautolisp, --lax        | once per run (a single flag)          | no                                     |
| [vendor-operator]      | the owner's dialect, --lax | once per operator per session         | yes                                    |
| [version-operator]     | (version gates)            | once per operator per session         | yes                                    |
| [encoding-replacement] | --lax                      | once per (caller, operator) (MR !509) | no                                     |
| [printer-nil-file]     | bricscad, clautolisp, lax  | once per run (a single flag)          | no                                     |

[errno-setvar] and [printer-nil-file] (merged 2026-11-04, 2.3.88) follow
AGENTS.md case 2, not section 7: --clautolisp, the adopted vendor's dialect,
is silent. That is B.2 again.

Questions to settle:
- what "the caller" is: the innermost user DEFUN, as
  %portability-warning-function-name gives it, and what it is at top level
  (MR !509 keys every top-level call under NIL, so all of a file's
  top-level calls share one warning);
- whether "first time" is per session, per run or per LOAD;
- whether *AUTOLISP-WARN-OUT-OF-DIALECT* should silence every divergence
  warning or only the out-of-dialect operator ones (it says "out of
  dialect", which a divergence is not);
- then align the 32 registered warnings on one helper, rather than each
  emitter carrying its own policy.

** B.4 "The behaviour of the selected dialect" cannot cover 2.1

Section 7's introduction says clautolisp gives the selected dialect's
behaviour. For 2.1 the products CRASH (reverse of an improper list on
BricsCAD, command-s refusing input on AutoCAD). The table gives only the
clautolisp column ("signal an error"). What do --dialect autocad / bricscad do
there? Section 5 item 3 proposes a [vendor-crash] diagnostic: presumably
signal plus that warning, but section 7 should say so.

** B.5 Section 2.7 text is stale

Section 2.7 still says "clautolisp signals: WRITE-LINE ... cannot be
encoded". Since 2.3.89 it writes per the dialect (low byte / best fit /
\U+XXXX) and warns. Left untouched by MR !509 because the section sits in
pjb's unpushed commit 9cf7f953.

** B.6 Section 4 items whose clautolisp behaviour predates section 7

Section 4 lists divergences already handled elsewhere. Each was decided before
section 7, and some contradict it:
- clautolisp-error-silent-under-autocad.issue: a dialect that is silent where
  section 7 wants the vendor's behaviour plus a warning;
- vl-exit-outside-vlx-caught-diverges.issue,
  comparison-operators-vendor-semantics.issue,
  numeric-edges-vendor-divergences.issue: these were written under the AGENTS.md
  case 2 / case 3 rules (B.2). Each needs re-reading once B.1 to B.3 are
  decided.
